1
00:00:16,287 --> 00:00:18,998
MACHI MORI - CUSTOMS OFFICIAL

2
00:00:26,756 --> 00:00:28,424
CLEAN UP EATING AREA

3
00:00:28,508 --> 00:00:29,968
DARWIN OCE 720

4
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:39,394
COUNSELOR

5
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:43,731
MIDORI SUGIHARA

6
00:00:54,159 --> 00:00:55,577
‎KEIICHIRO HAYAMI - IMMIGRATION OFFICIAL

7
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:56,953
Arima.

8
00:00:57,036 --> 00:00:58,413
It's been a while, Hayami.

9
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:00,582
Are you doing well?

10
00:01:00,665 --> 00:01:02,208
GATE 24 CONCEPT

11
00:01:03,042 --> 00:01:04,502
What are you calling about?

12
00:01:04,586 --> 00:01:06,337
I need a favor.

13
00:01:06,421 --> 00:01:08,882
I want you to find a spy among your team.

14
00:01:10,049 --> 00:01:11,259
What do you mean?

15
00:01:11,342 --> 00:01:14,137
According to the Cabinet
Intelligence and Research Office,

16
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:17,557
the number of foreign subversives
entering the country has surged

17
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:19,642
over the past two months.

18
00:01:19,726 --> 00:01:21,060
The past two months?

19
00:01:22,103 --> 00:01:26,107
In short, ever since that gate opened.

20
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:29,485
Gate 24 has become
a backdoor for terrorists.

21
00:01:29,569 --> 00:01:31,613
That's how they see it.

22
00:01:31,696 --> 00:01:33,573
There's a spy in Gate 24?

23
00:01:35,825 --> 00:01:39,203
I want you to find that spy.
Naturally, you'll be rewarded.

24
00:01:39,287 --> 00:01:41,414
People are starting
to recognize your efforts.

25
00:01:41,998 --> 00:01:46,127
Depending on the results,
I can back your return to headquarters.

26
00:01:46,210 --> 00:01:47,670
I'm counting on you, Hayami.

27
00:02:05,271 --> 00:02:06,689
Mori.

28
00:02:07,649 --> 00:02:08,650
Oh my.

29
00:02:10,568 --> 00:02:11,653
This stuff's great, isn't it?

30
00:02:11,736 --> 00:02:12,612
It's really delicious.

31
00:02:12,695 --> 00:02:14,197
Why have we never been here before?

32
00:02:15,156 --> 00:02:17,492
-I would love to go here on my own.
-Huh?

33
00:02:18,826 --> 00:02:20,161
What's up, Matsuyama?

34
00:02:20,245 --> 00:02:21,621
-Is something wrong with that drawing?
-Isn't this

35
00:02:21,704 --> 00:02:23,081
a drawing by Nobody?

36
00:02:23,748 --> 00:02:24,582
JUNKO TAMAI - CUSTOMS OFFICIAL

37
00:02:24,666 --> 00:02:26,751
Ah, you're right! It looks just like it.

38
00:02:26,834 --> 00:02:28,544
What's Nobody?

39
00:02:28,628 --> 00:02:29,587
You don't know?

40
00:02:29,671 --> 00:02:33,049
It's the elusive masked artist
who flies around the world.

41
00:02:33,132 --> 00:02:34,008
It's this.

42
00:02:35,051 --> 00:02:37,303
Nobody is a pacifist artist

43
00:02:37,387 --> 00:02:41,224
who paints artwork promoting peace
in war zones around the world.

44
00:02:41,307 --> 00:02:43,059
Wait, then this drawing is theirs?

45
00:02:43,142 --> 00:02:44,602
If it's real, wouldn't it
be worth a fortune?

46
00:02:44,686 --> 00:02:48,147
Well, isn't the touch a bit different?

47
00:02:48,231 --> 00:02:50,858
Nobody's lines are bolder
yet delicate, so to speak.

48
00:02:50,942 --> 00:02:52,276
Listen to Mr. Expert over here.

49
00:02:52,360 --> 00:02:55,363
Sorry, could you wipe that off?

50
00:02:55,446 --> 00:02:56,739
What? Should we?

51
00:02:56,823 --> 00:02:59,742
-What if this is--
-My niece drew it yesterday.

52
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:02,620
-Your niece…
-What?

53
00:03:03,246 --> 00:03:06,332
No way Nobody would come
to a soba shop like this.

54
00:03:06,416 --> 00:03:07,333
What's wrong with my shop?

55
00:03:07,417 --> 00:03:08,668
I meant it in a good way.

56
00:03:09,335 --> 00:03:12,880
So there's a spy amongst us?

57
00:03:15,508 --> 00:03:16,342
Well…

58
00:03:16,426 --> 00:03:17,760
…a soba place like this…

59
00:03:17,844 --> 00:03:20,013
What I know for sure is,
you guys are being suspected.

60
00:03:27,645 --> 00:03:29,188
Attention, please.

61
00:03:29,272 --> 00:03:34,444
Euro Sky Airways flight 638 from Astoria

62
00:03:34,527 --> 00:03:36,529
will arrive at Gate 24.

63
00:03:39,282 --> 00:03:40,116
-Light.
-Here.

64
00:03:40,199 --> 00:03:42,285
Man, you guys are lifesavers.

65
00:03:43,369 --> 00:03:44,620
We can handle it from here.

66
00:03:44,704 --> 00:03:47,623
-Ah, thank you, then.
-Yup.

67
00:03:50,251 --> 00:03:52,045
Thank you.

68
00:04:03,556 --> 00:04:05,350
-I'll take over.
-Okay.

69
00:04:05,433 --> 00:04:07,143
Excuse me. Thank you.

70
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:15,943
What should we do?

71
00:04:16,027 --> 00:04:17,111
What?

72
00:04:18,529 --> 00:04:22,909
How do we know if there's a spy or not?

73
00:04:26,037 --> 00:04:29,707
First, we should observe everyone closely.

74
00:04:36,672 --> 00:04:37,632
Over here.

75
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:41,594
-Here you are.
-Thank you.

76
00:04:41,677 --> 00:04:44,555
Fumio Kusamoto.

77
00:04:44,639 --> 00:04:45,807
Must have been a long flight.

78
00:04:45,890 --> 00:04:48,017
Man, I'm exhausted.

79
00:04:48,101 --> 00:04:51,771
Thirteen hours from Astoria
to Japan! Thirteen hours!

80
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:55,691
In that time, I could make
a whole lithograph.

81
00:04:57,276 --> 00:04:58,945
Ah, sorry, sorry.

82
00:04:59,028 --> 00:05:00,363
I'm a painter.

83
00:05:00,988 --> 00:05:01,989
A painter?

84
00:05:02,073 --> 00:05:03,116
That's my manager.

85
00:05:03,199 --> 00:05:04,617
‎JIM BROWN - MANAGER

86
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:08,746
Ah, those are all my tools of the trade,

87
00:05:08,830 --> 00:05:10,206
so handle them with care.

88
00:05:13,876 --> 00:05:16,295
-It's all right. Welcome back.
-Thanks.

89
00:05:16,379 --> 00:05:20,174
I think I'll relax and soak
in a hot spring for a bit.

90
00:05:45,408 --> 00:05:47,910
dozens of brushes, and many paintings too.

91
00:05:47,994 --> 00:05:50,872
But not all of it is art supplies, right?

92
00:05:50,955 --> 00:05:52,415
No, of course not.

93
00:05:53,207 --> 00:05:55,501
Since there's so much, I'd like
to inspect these in another room.

94
00:05:55,585 --> 00:05:57,128
Sure, go ahead.

95
00:05:58,171 --> 00:05:59,255
Please take over.

96
00:06:01,132 --> 00:06:02,508
Just over here.

97
00:06:04,343 --> 00:06:06,679
-I'm sorry, we're doing a quick check.
-Hey, what?

98
00:06:06,762 --> 00:06:08,389
-It won't be long.
-What?

99
00:06:10,308 --> 00:06:13,936
Kayla Ferguson and Mimi Ferguson, right?

100
00:06:14,020 --> 00:06:15,229
Yes.

101
00:06:15,313 --> 00:06:16,147
Please wait a moment.

102
00:06:22,653 --> 00:06:25,990
Um… You dropped this.

103
00:06:28,159 --> 00:06:30,286
-Oh, whoa!
-Mimi, what's wrong?

104
00:06:31,204 --> 00:06:33,456
-A-a-are you okay?
-Is something wrong?

105
00:06:34,832 --> 00:06:36,000
I'm a doctor.

106
00:06:36,083 --> 00:06:37,835
-Ah, a doctor…
-Let me take a look.

107
00:06:37,919 --> 00:06:39,045
Stay still a second.

108
00:06:40,713 --> 00:06:43,716
She seems to have
a slight fever. All right.

109
00:06:45,468 --> 00:06:46,802
I'll contact the quarantine official.

110
00:06:46,886 --> 00:06:48,137
Does it hurt anywhere?

111
00:06:49,805 --> 00:06:51,557
-This is Endo from Gate 24.
-Are you feeling uncomfortable?

112
00:06:51,641 --> 00:06:54,393
-There's a child who's not feeling well.
-Let's move you inside.

113
00:06:56,187 --> 00:06:57,063
Chika?

114
00:06:57,146 --> 00:07:01,817
There was a plane of passengers suspected
of having measles from Thailand,

115
00:07:01,901 --> 00:07:04,195
so all the quarantine staff
got pulled away to deal with them.

116
00:07:05,112 --> 00:07:08,074
-Could you wait a little longer?
-You'll speak with a doctor back here.

117
00:07:08,157 --> 00:07:09,909
-Thank you.
-Just here.

118
00:07:11,702 --> 00:07:12,537
Ah, over here.

119
00:07:14,956 --> 00:07:17,875
It looks like it'll be a while
until human quarantine is available.

120
00:07:17,959 --> 00:07:19,835
-Please bring the medical kit.
-Ah, yes.

121
00:07:19,919 --> 00:07:21,963
-Ma'am, support her neck.
-Yes.

122
00:07:22,046 --> 00:07:22,922
All right.

123
00:07:23,005 --> 00:07:23,965
Thank you.

124
00:07:24,048 --> 00:07:25,258
This might feel a bit cold.

125
00:07:26,634 --> 00:07:29,136
-What's your name?
-Mimi.

126
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:31,472
Mimi? That's a cute name.

127
00:07:33,015 --> 00:07:34,559
Will she be all right?

128
00:07:34,642 --> 00:07:37,770
She might just be exhausted
from the flight.

129
00:07:37,853 --> 00:07:39,397
She should recover after resting a bit.

130
00:07:40,022 --> 00:07:41,649
You mentioned you're medical staff.

131
00:07:41,732 --> 00:07:44,443
-Do you have any identification?
-Yes.

132
00:07:49,198 --> 00:07:50,950
Kazuo Shirosaki.

133
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:56,414
That Kazuo Shirosaki?

134
00:07:56,998 --> 00:07:57,873
Which one?

135
00:07:59,375 --> 00:08:00,251
Excuse me.

136
00:08:00,334 --> 00:08:01,669
Do you have any more ice packs?

137
00:08:02,253 --> 00:08:03,296
-Yes.
-Yes.

138
00:08:06,382 --> 00:08:08,759
Kazuo Shirosaki, 61 years old.

139
00:08:08,843 --> 00:08:10,595
A member of the NPO "Saving Life"

140
00:08:10,678 --> 00:08:14,307
that provides aid to areas
affected by emergencies.

141
00:08:14,390 --> 00:08:17,560
Their work continues
to bring hope to people

142
00:08:17,643 --> 00:08:19,937
affected by disasters and conflict.

143
00:08:20,438 --> 00:08:23,274
It's true. He's amazing.

144
00:08:32,908 --> 00:08:34,285
How much longer is this going to take?

145
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:36,495
We're almost finished.

146
00:08:36,579 --> 00:08:40,124
Do you really need to take
this long? It's just art supplies.

147
00:08:40,207 --> 00:08:41,667
It's regulation.

148
00:08:42,251 --> 00:08:44,086
Fine, just hurry up.

149
00:08:55,306 --> 00:08:56,641
What is this?

150
00:08:56,724 --> 00:08:59,060
-Vanilla beans.
-Vanilla beans?

151
00:08:59,727 --> 00:09:02,897
You mix them into the paint
to give it texture.

152
00:09:02,980 --> 00:09:03,981
Haven't you checked enough?

153
00:09:04,774 --> 00:09:06,067
I'm sorry, just a little longer.

154
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:12,073
I… I can't hold it anymore!

155
00:09:13,032 --> 00:09:13,908
-This way!
-Just a little further!

156
00:09:13,991 --> 00:09:15,368
-There, there!
-Right here!

157
00:09:15,451 --> 00:09:17,620
-Here, here.
-Here, here, here.

158
00:09:20,289 --> 00:09:22,333
He should've told us sooner
that his stomach hurt.

159
00:09:23,709 --> 00:09:25,336
-Ah, Matsuyama.
-Yes?

160
00:09:25,419 --> 00:09:27,421
-About his luggage…
-Ah,

161
00:09:27,505 --> 00:09:30,966
it'd be bad if we both left, right?
Seike, please wait here.

162
00:09:33,636 --> 00:09:35,262
No, that's not what I meant.

163
00:09:43,479 --> 00:09:45,690
-Thank you for your help.
-It's nothing.

164
00:09:52,238 --> 00:09:55,241
I used to live in Astoria too.

165
00:09:56,575 --> 00:09:58,869
-Really?
-Yes,

166
00:09:58,953 --> 00:10:00,162
three years ago.

167
00:10:00,246 --> 00:10:01,997
Did you work at the airport
over there too?

168
00:10:02,081 --> 00:10:04,583
No, I worked at a museum.

169
00:10:04,667 --> 00:10:06,252
-The one in the old town area?
-Yes.

170
00:10:08,045 --> 00:10:10,381
My daughter loved that museum.

171
00:10:11,006 --> 00:10:13,008
Every time we went, it was spotless.

172
00:10:14,385 --> 00:10:16,137
I polished it every day.

173
00:10:18,514 --> 00:10:19,765
You did?

174
00:10:20,391 --> 00:10:21,225
Yes.

175
00:10:21,308 --> 00:10:22,518
Mr. Shirosaki?

176
00:10:22,601 --> 00:10:24,103
She wants to talk to you.

177
00:10:27,898 --> 00:10:28,733
What's wrong?

178
00:10:28,816 --> 00:10:30,776
My tummy hurts.

179
00:10:31,527 --> 00:10:32,361
Your stomach?

180
00:10:39,034 --> 00:10:42,371
We apologize, operations
are temporarily suspended.

181
00:10:42,455 --> 00:10:44,623
Please wait until we receive confirmation.

182
00:10:46,333 --> 00:10:48,669
Hey, did something happen?

183
00:10:48,753 --> 00:10:50,713
Where's Jim?

184
00:10:50,796 --> 00:10:53,048
I'm sorry. Please wait a moment.

185
00:10:55,885 --> 00:10:59,472
-What's going on?
-Multiple people have upset stomachs.

186
00:10:59,555 --> 00:11:00,514
Excuse me.

187
00:11:01,140 --> 00:11:04,059
The police have something to share.

188
00:11:04,143 --> 00:11:07,772
We received an informal report
from the Astoria airport police.

189
00:11:08,439 --> 00:11:10,858
A few hours ago, a food poisoning incident

190
00:11:10,941 --> 00:11:13,861
broke out at the Astoria airport.

191
00:11:13,944 --> 00:11:16,363
People who ate at airport restaurants

192
00:11:16,447 --> 00:11:20,117
are reporting headaches and stomach pain
and are being rushed to hospitals.

193
00:11:20,201 --> 00:11:22,745
-Whoa, that's crazy.
-That's terrible.

194
00:11:22,828 --> 00:11:24,580
Considering the onset time,

195
00:11:24,663 --> 00:11:28,584
passengers on the flight from Astoria
might experience the same symptoms.

196
00:11:28,667 --> 00:11:30,920
Actually, people are already
showing symptoms.

197
00:11:31,003 --> 00:11:34,715
Yes, right now, a seven-year-old girl
and a male in his thirties

198
00:11:34,798 --> 00:11:35,966
are complaining of stomach pain.

199
00:11:36,050 --> 00:11:37,051
So it's true then.

200
00:11:37,134 --> 00:11:38,177
What's the cause?

201
00:11:38,260 --> 00:11:40,554
They haven't identified it yet.

202
00:11:40,638 --> 00:11:43,182
There are too many restaurants.
They don't know which one caused it.

203
00:11:43,849 --> 00:11:46,727
If it's spoiled ingredients,
it's an accident,

204
00:11:46,810 --> 00:11:50,940
but if someone intentionally
mixed something in,

205
00:11:51,565 --> 00:11:52,566
it's a crime.

206
00:11:53,442 --> 00:11:55,861
An accident or a crime…

207
00:11:55,945 --> 00:11:57,821
We should check
on other passengers as well.

208
00:11:57,905 --> 00:11:59,281
RAN FUKAZAWA - CHIEF HEARING OFFICER

209
00:11:59,365 --> 00:12:00,741
How many passengers remain?

210
00:12:00,824 --> 00:12:02,034
About another 15.

211
00:12:02,785 --> 00:12:04,912
Halt all inspections for now.

212
00:12:04,995 --> 00:12:08,249
Split up and check
the health of the passengers.

213
00:12:08,332 --> 00:12:11,418
Move those who are clear
to the waiting area.

214
00:12:11,502 --> 00:12:13,170
We also need to check those
who already cleared immigration.

215
00:12:13,254 --> 00:12:15,839
-Yes. Notify all relevant divisions.
-Yes, sir.

216
00:12:15,923 --> 00:12:19,009
-All right, we will contact Astoria again.
-Yes.

217
00:12:19,093 --> 00:12:21,554
I'll notify the Counselor myself.

218
00:12:21,637 --> 00:12:23,514
All right, let's go, go, go.

219
00:12:25,683 --> 00:12:27,351
I am a quarantine official.

220
00:12:27,434 --> 00:12:30,729
I will be asking you all
a few questions about your health.

221
00:12:30,813 --> 00:12:32,940
If any of you are feeling ill,
please let us know immediately.

222
00:12:33,607 --> 00:12:36,068
-Thank you very much.
-Please proceed over there.

223
00:12:37,611 --> 00:12:40,239
-Please go on to the waiting area.
-Just over there.

224
00:12:40,322 --> 00:12:41,657
You first, then.

225
00:12:41,740 --> 00:12:42,658
HAND SANITIZER

226
00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:52,418
He's the last one, then.

227
00:12:56,547 --> 00:12:57,423
Ah, no…

228
00:12:59,675 --> 00:13:01,343
I'll tell him.

229
00:13:09,059 --> 00:13:11,020
I think I'm feeling a little better now.

230
00:13:11,103 --> 00:13:12,730
That's good to hear.

231
00:13:12,813 --> 00:13:14,982
A medical professional
is in a separate room,

232
00:13:15,065 --> 00:13:16,900
-so we'll have you see him.
-Okay.

233
00:13:16,984 --> 00:13:19,278
We'll bring your luggage there as well.

234
00:13:19,361 --> 00:13:21,655
All right. Oh, dear.

235
00:13:21,739 --> 00:13:23,907
I was supposed to fly back
to the UK tomorrow.

236
00:13:23,991 --> 00:13:25,659
I see.

237
00:13:31,290 --> 00:13:32,333
Sorry.

238
00:13:37,671 --> 00:13:38,881
Huh…

239
00:13:39,423 --> 00:13:40,507
What's wrong?

240
00:13:42,760 --> 00:13:43,594
‎Nothing.

241
00:13:54,188 --> 00:13:57,441
-So?
-Looks like he's healthy.

242
00:13:59,193 --> 00:14:00,194
What happened?

243
00:14:00,903 --> 00:14:02,571
Oh, no, nothing really.

244
00:14:05,741 --> 00:14:08,494
-Hey, I wanted to ask you something.
-Yes?

245
00:14:08,577 --> 00:14:10,954
My inspection's done, right?

246
00:14:11,038 --> 00:14:12,498
Can I go get Jim's luggage?

247
00:14:13,290 --> 00:14:15,542
Jim's luggage hasn't
finished inspection yet.

248
00:14:15,626 --> 00:14:18,462
Yes. But the stuff inside belongs to me.

249
00:14:18,545 --> 00:14:21,465
Even so, the owner who checked
the luggage must be present.

250
00:14:21,548 --> 00:14:24,134
Yes, I understand,
but can't you make an exception?

251
00:14:24,677 --> 00:14:25,761
I'm very sorry, but no.

252
00:14:25,844 --> 00:14:29,014
What? Fine.

253
00:14:29,890 --> 00:14:31,892
By-the-book bureaucrats.

254
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:39,942
I'll place your luggage here.

255
00:14:40,693 --> 00:14:41,652
‎Thank you.

256
00:14:42,611 --> 00:14:44,822
This man also seems to have stomach pain.

257
00:14:44,905 --> 00:14:47,074
Would you mind taking a look at him?

258
00:14:47,157 --> 00:14:49,994
Okay. Please take off
your shoes and lay down.

259
00:14:50,077 --> 00:14:50,911
‎Okay.

260
00:14:55,457 --> 00:14:59,378
Um… Matsuyama.

261
00:15:04,133 --> 00:15:07,761
The art supplies in that suitcase
belong to Kusamoto, right?

262
00:15:07,845 --> 00:15:09,304
Yes, that's what he said.

263
00:15:11,306 --> 00:15:12,266
It's strange.

264
00:15:13,517 --> 00:15:14,351
What is?

265
00:15:15,561 --> 00:15:18,188
Kusamoto is Nobody, the anonymous artist.

266
00:15:18,981 --> 00:15:20,607
-Huh?
-Nobody?

267
00:15:20,691 --> 00:15:22,484
-For real?
-No, no, no…

268
00:15:23,318 --> 00:15:26,030
-Hey, Mori, what are you talking about?
-Yes.

269
00:15:26,113 --> 00:15:27,740
We were talking about it in the soba shop,

270
00:15:27,823 --> 00:15:29,366
but there's no way
they'd actually be here now.

271
00:15:29,950 --> 00:15:31,910
But it's the only thing that makes sense.

272
00:15:31,994 --> 00:15:35,664
But I can't believe Nobody
is just a Japanese man…

273
00:15:35,748 --> 00:15:37,791
Nobody is anonymous.
No reason he can't be Japanese.

274
00:15:37,875 --> 00:15:39,793
-Do you have proof?
-Ah, yes.

275
00:15:39,877 --> 00:15:42,212
Two days ago, a mural believed to be

276
00:15:42,296 --> 00:15:45,049
painted by Nobody was discovered
on a building in downtown Astoria.

277
00:15:45,924 --> 00:15:46,925
This.

278
00:15:49,053 --> 00:15:51,764
The exact same drawing
was inside that sketchbook.

279
00:15:55,642 --> 00:15:56,643
Huh…

280
00:16:04,693 --> 00:16:05,819
Is that really him?

281
00:16:05,903 --> 00:16:08,155
So he painted it two days ago in Astoria

282
00:16:08,238 --> 00:16:09,907
and returned to Japan today?

283
00:16:10,908 --> 00:16:13,035
The timing matches.

284
00:16:13,118 --> 00:16:15,245
Isn't that a bit too wild
of an assumption?

285
00:16:15,954 --> 00:16:18,332
Seike, you were the one
who inspected Kusamoto, right?

286
00:16:18,415 --> 00:16:20,751
-Yes.
-Did you notice anything of interest?

287
00:16:21,794 --> 00:16:24,129
-Of interest?
-If he really is Nobody,

288
00:16:24,213 --> 00:16:26,006
he should have frequent travel
to politically unstable countries.

289
00:16:26,924 --> 00:16:28,550
Well, I didn't look that far into him.

290
00:16:28,634 --> 00:16:30,219
Must be tough though,

291
00:16:30,302 --> 00:16:33,013
flying around the world
just to paint murals.

292
00:16:33,597 --> 00:16:35,390
It seems he usually resides in the UK.

293
00:16:35,474 --> 00:16:36,725
The UK?

294
00:16:36,809 --> 00:16:38,852
Come to think of it, Jim mentioned he was

295
00:16:38,936 --> 00:16:40,562
planning to return to the UK tomorrow.

296
00:16:40,646 --> 00:16:42,314
That's strange.

297
00:16:42,397 --> 00:16:46,693
Kusamoto said he planned to relax
at a Japanese hot spring for a while.

298
00:16:46,777 --> 00:16:50,447
Their stories don't align.

299
00:16:50,531 --> 00:16:52,866
Truly an anonymous painter.
His actions are mysterious too.

300
00:16:54,076 --> 00:16:56,995
Could that rumor actually be true?

301
00:16:58,997 --> 00:17:02,501
It's a rumor floating around online.
The theory that Nobody is a spy.

302
00:17:02,584 --> 00:17:03,585
A spy?

303
00:17:04,169 --> 00:17:06,213
The rumor says that though
he pretends to advocate

304
00:17:06,296 --> 00:17:08,215
for the weak, he's actually
gathering military

305
00:17:08,298 --> 00:17:10,717
and government intelligence
in conflict zones.

306
00:17:11,343 --> 00:17:13,762
Oh, come on, don't believe such nonsense.

307
00:17:13,846 --> 00:17:16,056
But wasn't there a theory that even

308
00:17:16,140 --> 00:17:19,059
Matsuo Basho was actually a spy?

309
00:17:19,143 --> 00:17:20,561
The Narrow Road to the Deep North.

310
00:17:20,644 --> 00:17:24,523
Thinking like that, it makes sense why
he was in politically unstable Astoria.

311
00:17:24,606 --> 00:17:26,275
Can we really just let
someone like that pass through?

312
00:17:26,358 --> 00:17:28,902
But we don't have any concrete evidence.

313
00:17:28,986 --> 00:17:30,445
Exactly.

314
00:17:30,529 --> 00:17:34,366
Isn't it premature to assume
Kusamoto is Nobody?

315
00:17:41,665 --> 00:17:43,417
Let's get back to work!

316
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:45,627
Let's go, go, go.

317
00:17:48,964 --> 00:17:51,133
Mori, can I speak with you for a moment?

318
00:17:56,763 --> 00:18:00,434
What Matsuyama mentioned
actually caught my attention.

319
00:18:00,517 --> 00:18:01,351
Yes.

320
00:18:01,435 --> 00:18:04,605
If Kusamoto is Nobody, as you say,

321
00:18:04,688 --> 00:18:07,816
and if he's a spy, as Matsuyama says,

322
00:18:09,276 --> 00:18:10,319
there may be someone

323
00:18:10,402 --> 00:18:13,363
amongst us who's
in direct contact with him.

324
00:18:15,032 --> 00:18:19,244
If there really is a spy in Gate 24,

325
00:18:19,328 --> 00:18:20,871
it may be that person.

326
00:18:54,238 --> 00:18:55,572
What are you doing?

327
00:18:57,241 --> 00:18:58,742
What are you hiding?

328
00:19:00,035 --> 00:19:01,828
I'm not hiding anything.

329
00:19:01,912 --> 00:19:04,957
What did Kusamoto write in that notebook?

330
00:19:05,874 --> 00:19:06,792
Kusamoto?

331
00:19:07,751 --> 00:19:09,920
-What are you talking about?
-Stop playing dumb.

332
00:19:11,463 --> 00:19:12,506
Seike,

333
00:19:13,090 --> 00:19:17,094
you knew Kusamoto
was the anonymous artist, didn't you?

334
00:19:21,181 --> 00:19:22,266
Seike.

335
00:19:24,726 --> 00:19:28,772
You knew Kusamoto
was the anonymous artist, didn't you?

336
00:19:29,398 --> 00:19:30,774
Seike.

337
00:19:33,610 --> 00:19:34,695
I'm sorry.

338
00:19:36,029 --> 00:19:37,114
I have

339
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:39,992
done something

340
00:19:41,368 --> 00:19:42,452
an immigration official should never do.

341
00:19:59,136 --> 00:20:00,137
This is…

342
00:20:04,516 --> 00:20:06,018
Oh my.

343
00:20:15,235 --> 00:20:16,820
What's this?

344
00:20:19,698 --> 00:20:20,824
It's the autograph of

345
00:20:22,492 --> 00:20:23,618
Nobody, the anonymous artist.

346
00:20:24,661 --> 00:20:25,829
Nobody's autograph?

347
00:20:26,705 --> 00:20:30,334
So you noticed he was Nobody, Seike?

348
00:20:31,626 --> 00:20:32,502
Yes.

349
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:35,881
When I was inspecting Jim's luggage…

350
00:20:40,510 --> 00:20:44,014
Thank you for waiting.
Could you please sign here?

351
00:20:47,351 --> 00:20:51,355
By any chance, are you Nobody?

352
00:20:51,438 --> 00:20:52,314
What?

353
00:20:53,065 --> 00:20:54,441
What are you talking about?

354
00:20:55,275 --> 00:20:58,070
Don't play dumb. You can't fool me.

355
00:21:02,199 --> 00:21:03,742
Please keep this confidential.

356
00:21:06,745 --> 00:21:09,539
So, in exchange for keeping quiet,
you got his autograph?

357
00:21:09,623 --> 00:21:11,541
This is work!

358
00:21:11,625 --> 00:21:15,295
What was the purpose
of establishing this gate again?

359
00:21:15,379 --> 00:21:17,631
Sharing information
across Immigration and Customs.

360
00:21:17,714 --> 00:21:19,174
Information sharing!

361
00:21:19,257 --> 00:21:21,635
We shouldn't have to tell you that
at this point.

362
00:21:21,718 --> 00:21:22,719
I'm sorry.

363
00:21:22,803 --> 00:21:24,805
What a wild swing and a miss, huh?

364
00:21:25,555 --> 00:21:27,182
Seike,

365
00:21:27,265 --> 00:21:29,184
-you are an embarrassment!
-Hayami,

366
00:21:29,810 --> 00:21:35,399
so there is a spy amongst us here?

367
00:21:38,985 --> 00:21:39,820
Excuse me.

368
00:21:41,238 --> 00:21:42,406
Oh, thank goodness.

369
00:21:42,489 --> 00:21:43,490
It's from Chief Hearing Officer Fukazawa.

370
00:21:45,742 --> 00:21:46,952
Hello. This is Seike.

371
00:21:49,079 --> 00:21:51,456
What? Hey, everyone.

372
00:21:52,457 --> 00:21:55,836
Regarding the food poisoning
at Astoria airport,

373
00:21:55,919 --> 00:21:57,879
according to local police,

374
00:21:57,963 --> 00:22:01,716
it's very likely some sort of poison
was scattered in an airport restaurant.

375
00:22:02,300 --> 00:22:03,760
Poison?

376
00:22:03,844 --> 00:22:07,305
It's a restaurant called Freedom.

377
00:22:07,889 --> 00:22:11,393
Please ask each passenger whether they ate

378
00:22:11,476 --> 00:22:13,437
at that restaurant.

379
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:14,396
-Yes!
-Yes.

380
00:22:17,065 --> 00:22:19,985
Everyone, I'd like to ask
you all something.

381
00:22:22,612 --> 00:22:25,490
-What? J-Jim, a-are you okay?
-Jim!

382
00:22:25,574 --> 00:22:28,034
Jim, can you hear me?
Ventricular fibrillation.

383
00:22:28,785 --> 00:22:30,537
-Bring me an AED.
-Okay!

384
00:22:30,620 --> 00:22:32,038
Help me lower him to the flower.

385
00:22:32,122 --> 00:22:33,206
-Okay!
-Here we go.

386
00:22:33,874 --> 00:22:35,208
I understand.

387
00:22:37,460 --> 00:22:40,964
One male passenger
has been transported to the hospital.

388
00:22:41,047 --> 00:22:42,215
What's his condition?

389
00:22:42,299 --> 00:22:45,594
Thanks to swift emergency treatment,
his life isn't in danger.

390
00:22:45,677 --> 00:22:47,304
Ah, thank goodness.

391
00:22:48,096 --> 00:22:50,974
But your gate is one trouble

392
00:22:51,057 --> 00:22:52,392
-after another.
-Save the lecture for later.

393
00:22:52,475 --> 00:22:55,770
Right now, understanding
the situation comes first.

394
00:22:55,854 --> 00:22:57,731
I have a good idea of the situation.

395
00:22:57,814 --> 00:23:00,275
This is most likely terrorism.

396
00:23:01,109 --> 00:23:01,985
Terrorism?

397
00:23:02,569 --> 00:23:05,030
Ever since the regime change
two years ago in Astoria,

398
00:23:05,113 --> 00:23:08,158
an anti-government organization
called Wings of the People

399
00:23:08,241 --> 00:23:10,702
has been committing
frequent acts of terror.

400
00:23:10,785 --> 00:23:12,204
Wings of the People?

401
00:23:12,287 --> 00:23:16,166
-Looking at the situation, it's likely--
-I'm not so sure about that.

402
00:23:16,249 --> 00:23:20,170
At this stage, there's no evidence
linking this to that group.

403
00:23:24,174 --> 00:23:26,635
-A restaurant called Freedom?
-Yes.

404
00:23:26,718 --> 00:23:29,471
Did you eat there before departure?

405
00:23:30,055 --> 00:23:31,014
No, I didn't.

406
00:23:31,097 --> 00:23:33,600
We did. Why?

407
00:23:34,434 --> 00:23:38,855
Um, it's possible
the food there was poisoned.

408
00:23:39,481 --> 00:23:42,192
Is that why Mimi's sick?
But I ate there too.

409
00:23:42,817 --> 00:23:45,820
Symptoms vary depending on the amount
consumed and your constitution.

410
00:23:45,904 --> 00:23:47,489
So does that mean I…

411
00:23:47,572 --> 00:23:51,201
You might not show symptoms,
but we shouldn't be overly optimistic.

412
00:23:53,703 --> 00:23:55,372
Do you remember what you ate?

413
00:23:56,539 --> 00:24:01,836
Uh, it was a buffet, so we ate
some salad, chicken…

414
00:24:03,713 --> 00:24:06,299
-Excuse me.
-Yes?

415
00:24:07,259 --> 00:24:09,928
Hey! Is it true Jim
was taken to the hospital?

416
00:24:10,011 --> 00:24:12,472
Yes, his condition suddenly deteriorated.

417
00:24:12,555 --> 00:24:14,724
He had stabilized briefly, but…

418
00:24:15,350 --> 00:24:16,559
Then why?

419
00:24:17,102 --> 00:24:19,229
A poison may have been scattered

420
00:24:19,312 --> 00:24:21,815
at a restaurant called
Freedom in Astoria Airport.

421
00:24:21,898 --> 00:24:23,024
-Poison?
-Yes.

422
00:24:23,108 --> 00:24:25,402
That's why we need to ask you
some questions as well.

423
00:24:25,485 --> 00:24:28,280
As you can see, I'm perfectly fine!

424
00:24:28,363 --> 00:24:30,323
More importantly, which hospital
was Jim taken to?

425
00:24:30,991 --> 00:24:33,326
I need to go to him right away.

426
00:24:34,536 --> 00:24:36,246
I'll take those.

427
00:24:36,329 --> 00:24:38,748
-Ah, no, no, you can't…
-Excuse me.

428
00:24:38,832 --> 00:24:40,250
Then what do I do?

429
00:24:40,750 --> 00:24:43,628
Leave them here until Jim gets back?

430
00:24:43,712 --> 00:24:45,880
-No, um, like I said…
-Hey!

431
00:24:45,964 --> 00:24:46,840
Shut up!

432
00:24:50,927 --> 00:24:52,429
Don't take it out on a child!

433
00:24:56,975 --> 00:25:00,520
Right now, your job
is to answer the questions.

434
00:25:01,229 --> 00:25:02,522
You ate with Jim, didn't you?

435
00:25:03,273 --> 00:25:04,274
Shirosaki.

436
00:25:08,445 --> 00:25:09,321
I ate lunch

437
00:25:10,196 --> 00:25:12,240
at a different restaurant.

438
00:25:13,283 --> 00:25:15,660
Jim was the only one
who went to that place.

439
00:25:16,286 --> 00:25:17,120
Satisfied?

440
00:25:17,954 --> 00:25:19,581
Thank you for your cooperation.

441
00:25:29,257 --> 00:25:30,717
Don't cry, don't cry.

442
00:25:31,509 --> 00:25:32,886
It's okay now.

443
00:25:43,563 --> 00:25:45,065
This isn't terrorism.

444
00:25:47,692 --> 00:25:50,487
-What?
-I saw the news online.

445
00:25:51,071 --> 00:25:53,782
They suspect it was caused
by the anti-government organization

446
00:25:54,491 --> 00:25:56,701
Wings of the People.

447
00:25:57,327 --> 00:25:58,244
But they aren't

448
00:25:58,828 --> 00:26:01,247
an organization that
indiscriminately involves civilians.

449
00:26:02,457 --> 00:26:05,627
If anything, we should suspect
the government.

450
00:26:05,710 --> 00:26:08,671
Astoria is on the brink
of economic collapse

451
00:26:08,755 --> 00:26:10,882
due to inflation.

452
00:26:10,965 --> 00:26:15,345
Meanwhile, officials and politicians
line their pockets through corruption.

453
00:26:16,096 --> 00:26:18,723
The public's frustration
has reached its limit.

454
00:26:19,391 --> 00:26:21,768
Lives exist to be saved.

455
00:26:23,311 --> 00:26:26,481
But in this world,
there are lives not worth saving.

456
00:26:29,025 --> 00:26:30,819
Seeing the tragedy in Astoria

457
00:26:32,112 --> 00:26:33,530
has made me feel that way.

458
00:26:35,115 --> 00:26:36,616
Excuse me.

459
00:26:37,992 --> 00:26:40,036
The man gave it to me.

460
00:26:46,292 --> 00:26:49,045
By "the man", do you mean Jim?

461
00:26:49,129 --> 00:26:50,463
Yes.

462
00:27:00,223 --> 00:27:02,559
He gave you this at the restaurant, right?

463
00:27:03,226 --> 00:27:04,978
-Yes.
-I'm sure.

464
00:27:05,061 --> 00:27:06,312
I took a photo at the restaurant.

465
00:27:07,772 --> 00:27:08,982
A photo?

466
00:27:19,659 --> 00:27:22,245
-He handed her a napkin?
-Yes.

467
00:27:23,413 --> 00:27:24,956
There's something I want to confirm.

468
00:27:30,670 --> 00:27:33,923
I finally get those back, huh?

469
00:27:34,757 --> 00:27:37,510
All right, let's get this
over with already.

470
00:27:38,219 --> 00:27:39,929
Before that, we need to confirm something.

471
00:27:40,805 --> 00:27:42,098
Checking again?

472
00:27:45,226 --> 00:27:49,105
Are you really Nobody?

473
00:27:51,524 --> 00:27:52,442
What?

474
00:27:59,073 --> 00:28:00,408
What's that?

475
00:28:00,492 --> 00:28:05,788
That girl, Mimi, got this from Jim.

476
00:28:06,706 --> 00:28:09,250
From Jim? Why would he do that?

477
00:28:16,591 --> 00:28:21,054
It seems he gave her this
at a restaurant called Freedom.

478
00:28:21,804 --> 00:28:22,931
‎Hold on.

479
00:28:28,895 --> 00:28:29,729
‎Here you are.

480
00:28:34,150 --> 00:28:36,319
Alright, I'm sure you're starving, right?

481
00:28:36,402 --> 00:28:37,737
I got a bunch.

482
00:28:39,197 --> 00:28:44,369
Jim is the one who drew this. Not you.

483
00:28:46,162 --> 00:28:48,414
In short, well, in other words,

484
00:28:49,207 --> 00:28:51,167
you are the manager,

485
00:28:51,835 --> 00:28:54,963
and the real Nobody is Jim.

486
00:28:55,046 --> 00:28:57,549
-No way…
-What?

487
00:28:57,632 --> 00:28:59,509
Their roles were reversed?

488
00:29:00,385 --> 00:29:01,594
What?

489
00:29:02,846 --> 00:29:05,640
I see! I see, I see.

490
00:29:05,723 --> 00:29:06,975
That's funny, isn't it?

491
00:29:07,809 --> 00:29:10,395
Nobody didn't want his identity known,

492
00:29:10,478 --> 00:29:14,190
so he switched roles
of painter and manager with you.

493
00:29:15,149 --> 00:29:16,442
Is that correct?

494
00:29:17,735 --> 00:29:20,530
Would Nobody, who fights for the weak,

495
00:29:20,613 --> 00:29:22,866
yell at a child like that?

496
00:29:22,949 --> 00:29:24,826
No, um, like I said…

497
00:29:24,909 --> 00:29:26,202
-Hey!
-Shut up!

498
00:29:26,286 --> 00:29:28,872
That's also what drew our suspicion.

499
00:29:30,707 --> 00:29:33,042
So what if that's true? Huh?

500
00:29:34,669 --> 00:29:35,670
That's right.

501
00:29:36,462 --> 00:29:39,841
He asked me to play Nobody for him.

502
00:29:40,425 --> 00:29:42,051
I've been doing so for years now.

503
00:29:42,760 --> 00:29:45,388
Is there a problem with that?

504
00:29:46,180 --> 00:29:47,140
No.

505
00:29:47,682 --> 00:29:49,559
That's not where the problem lies.

506
00:29:50,810 --> 00:29:52,020
What?

507
00:30:03,281 --> 00:30:06,159
This is the photo Mimi's mother took.

508
00:30:10,455 --> 00:30:12,498
-Excuse me.
-Wait, wait…

509
00:30:13,750 --> 00:30:15,919
This is you, isn't it?

510
00:30:16,002 --> 00:30:19,255
Yes, that does seem to be me.

511
00:30:19,339 --> 00:30:22,550
You said you didn't go to that restaurant

512
00:30:22,634 --> 00:30:25,303
and that only Jim ate there.

513
00:30:26,137 --> 00:30:28,139
-Why lie?
-No, it wasn't a lie.

514
00:30:28,222 --> 00:30:30,391
Um, I'm sorry, you know,

515
00:30:30,475 --> 00:30:31,476
I just

516
00:30:32,226 --> 00:30:35,396
-misremembered, is all.
-Another strange thing.

517
00:30:36,022 --> 00:30:38,816
Jim was rushed to the hospital
in critical condition.

518
00:30:39,567 --> 00:30:44,113
And yet, you never once worried
about your own health.

519
00:30:45,615 --> 00:30:46,908
So what?

520
00:30:46,991 --> 00:30:50,995
It's almost as if you knew you wouldn't
be affected by the poison.

521
00:30:52,246 --> 00:30:55,375
-Well, normally when you're sick--
-You are the one

522
00:30:56,501 --> 00:30:59,170
who poisoned the food
at that restaurant, aren't you?

523
00:30:59,963 --> 00:31:01,130
What?

524
00:31:01,214 --> 00:31:02,423
Seriously?

525
00:31:03,383 --> 00:31:07,512
What are you talking about?
That's not true!

526
00:31:09,597 --> 00:31:11,849
The restaurant was a buffet.

527
00:31:12,850 --> 00:31:14,894
The two who had stomach pain,

528
00:31:14,978 --> 00:31:17,355
Jim and Mimi,
both had salads on their plates,

529
00:31:17,438 --> 00:31:19,899
but you didn't take any salad.

530
00:31:21,442 --> 00:31:23,236
-That's just a coincidence.
-Furthermore,

531
00:31:23,903 --> 00:31:26,823
those are rubber gloves
sticking out of your pocket, right?

532
00:31:28,032 --> 00:31:31,285
You used them to handle
the toxic substance, didn't you?

533
00:31:31,369 --> 00:31:36,124
That's-- You-- You're pretty
stupid too, huh? That's not true!

534
00:31:37,166 --> 00:31:40,753
That's all just speculation, isn't it?

535
00:31:41,713 --> 00:31:43,965
Do you have proof? Huh?

536
00:31:44,632 --> 00:31:46,718
If I have poison, then where is it?

537
00:31:54,017 --> 00:31:56,894
You don't think it's inside there, do you?

538
00:31:57,603 --> 00:32:00,523
May we inspect the luggage again?

539
00:32:00,606 --> 00:32:03,943
What? You already looked, right?

540
00:32:06,821 --> 00:32:07,822
Do what you want.

541
00:32:08,865 --> 00:32:09,741
However,

542
00:32:10,867 --> 00:32:13,536
if you don't find anything,
be prepared for the consequences.

543
00:32:15,496 --> 00:32:17,540
-Be prepared!
-Okay.

544
00:32:19,417 --> 00:32:23,212
‎Well then, I will check the contents.

545
00:33:06,839 --> 00:33:11,135
-That's the last piece of luggage.
-This is nuts, isn't it?

546
00:33:27,527 --> 00:33:31,030
Um… What's this?

547
00:33:33,241 --> 00:33:36,702
Ah, those are vanilla beans.
They're used on paintbrushes.

548
00:33:38,037 --> 00:33:39,789
Vanilla beans…

549
00:33:42,875 --> 00:33:44,752
Jim said the same thing.

550
00:33:52,218 --> 00:33:53,636
Miss?

551
00:33:54,387 --> 00:33:56,139
Did you find the poison?

552
00:34:03,604 --> 00:34:04,689
Well then…

553
00:34:06,858 --> 00:34:09,819
You're all prepared, aren't you?

554
00:34:13,322 --> 00:34:19,829
I recorded this whole thing.

555
00:34:21,664 --> 00:34:26,919
I'll post online how airport staff
treated me like a criminal.

556
00:34:28,588 --> 00:34:30,965
I look forward to seeing what happens.

557
00:34:33,467 --> 00:34:36,679
I'm taking back my luggage now.
No objections to that, right?

558
00:34:39,015 --> 00:34:39,849
No.

559
00:35:01,329 --> 00:35:02,663
Um…

560
00:35:04,040 --> 00:35:05,333
There's no smell.

561
00:35:06,250 --> 00:35:07,126
What?

562
00:35:08,669 --> 00:35:11,881
This white part, it isn't vanillin.

563
00:35:12,465 --> 00:35:13,299
Vanillin?

564
00:35:13,382 --> 00:35:15,343
Vanillin is where the smell comes from.

565
00:35:15,426 --> 00:35:18,054
If this is vanillin,
it should smell like it.

566
00:35:18,846 --> 00:35:20,389
-But--
-That's enough.

567
00:35:20,473 --> 00:35:22,808
-Just close it already!
-What is that white part then?

568
00:35:24,977 --> 00:35:27,313
-It's mold.
-Mold?

569
00:35:27,939 --> 00:35:30,066
Certain molds that grow on vanilla beans

570
00:35:30,149 --> 00:35:33,110
produce a toxic substance
called mycotoxin.

571
00:35:34,278 --> 00:35:35,321
Meaning?

572
00:35:38,366 --> 00:35:39,408
This is

573
00:35:40,785 --> 00:35:42,787
the poison that hurt Jim, isn't it?

574
00:35:44,121 --> 00:35:48,125
Eventually, the poison detected
in Jim's system will be identified.

575
00:35:48,209 --> 00:35:50,920
If that matches the substance found

576
00:35:51,003 --> 00:35:53,130
-in these vanilla beans--
-Damn it, you bastard!

577
00:35:53,214 --> 00:35:54,090
Wait!

578
00:35:55,007 --> 00:35:56,926
I'm kidding. It's a joke, it's a joke.

579
00:35:57,009 --> 00:35:58,177
I'd never run.

580
00:36:02,181 --> 00:36:03,224
All right…

581
00:36:07,270 --> 00:36:08,271
Stop!

582
00:36:12,400 --> 00:36:14,151
If you eat that,

583
00:36:15,361 --> 00:36:17,196
you might destroy the evidence.

584
00:36:18,698 --> 00:36:20,491
But that poison

585
00:36:21,242 --> 00:36:24,370
causes acute liver failure.

586
00:36:24,453 --> 00:36:25,955
And…

587
00:36:27,290 --> 00:36:29,834
A-And?

588
00:36:31,877 --> 00:36:32,962
You will die.

589
00:36:37,091 --> 00:36:39,385
-You're bluffing.
-No. You will die.

590
00:36:39,468 --> 00:36:40,970
-No, I won't!
-You will.

591
00:36:42,638 --> 00:36:44,307
Damn it!

592
00:36:51,480 --> 00:36:52,440
Yes!

593
00:36:52,523 --> 00:36:54,150
‎I don't want to die!

594
00:36:54,233 --> 00:36:57,236
What a big old mess.

595
00:37:06,412 --> 00:37:09,415
Kusamoto had massive debts,

596
00:37:09,498 --> 00:37:13,377
so it seems he was trying
to sell off Jim's artwork.

597
00:37:13,461 --> 00:37:16,756
-So that's why he poisoned Jim?
-Yes.

598
00:37:17,381 --> 00:37:20,468
But why would he drag
other people into it too?

599
00:37:21,093 --> 00:37:24,597
If he went straight for Jim,
he'd be the first suspect.

600
00:37:24,680 --> 00:37:28,351
So he tried to disguise it
as an indiscriminate attack.

601
00:37:29,101 --> 00:37:30,853
That's so selfish.

602
00:37:32,021 --> 00:37:34,732
You were right, Counselor.

603
00:37:35,900 --> 00:37:38,277
At least this incident

604
00:37:38,361 --> 00:37:41,280
wasn't terrorism
by an anti-government organization.

605
00:37:43,032 --> 00:37:44,241
That's true.

606
00:37:45,785 --> 00:37:50,039
It's true that mycotoxins
cause severe liver inflammation.

607
00:37:50,122 --> 00:37:52,583
However, unless ingested
in massive quantities,

608
00:37:52,666 --> 00:37:54,210
it's rarely fatal.

609
00:37:55,961 --> 00:37:57,671
So what you said earlier…

610
00:37:58,339 --> 00:37:59,590
-You will die.
-No, I won't!

611
00:37:59,673 --> 00:38:00,591
You will.

612
00:38:01,509 --> 00:38:03,010
Yes, it was a bluff.

613
00:38:03,969 --> 00:38:04,929
Oh my.

614
00:38:05,930 --> 00:38:10,518
But that bluff solved the case
and saved the culprit's life.

615
00:38:11,519 --> 00:38:14,397
You are an amazing customs official.

616
00:38:20,111 --> 00:38:21,320
Thank you very much.

617
00:38:25,950 --> 00:38:26,992
So, what about Jim?

618
00:38:27,076 --> 00:38:29,370
He received treatment at the hospital
and is recovering.

619
00:38:29,453 --> 00:38:31,247
That's good to hear.

620
00:38:31,330 --> 00:38:33,165
Now I can rest easy.

621
00:38:33,791 --> 00:38:35,292
Dr. Shirosaki!

622
00:38:37,336 --> 00:38:38,921
Mimi!

623
00:38:39,004 --> 00:38:41,549
Thank you so much, Dr. Shirosaki.

624
00:38:41,632 --> 00:38:42,883
Is Mimi okay?

625
00:38:42,967 --> 00:38:45,469
Yes, but just to be sure,
we're heading to the hospital now.

626
00:38:46,637 --> 00:38:48,889
She really wanted you to have this.

627
00:38:49,640 --> 00:38:50,933
‎-Here.
‎-Huh?

628
00:38:51,517 --> 00:38:54,019
Wow, you're good.

629
00:38:57,523 --> 00:38:58,774
Thank you very much.

630
00:38:58,858 --> 00:39:00,734
-Shall we head outside together?
-Sure.

631
00:39:01,986 --> 00:39:04,196
Well then, I'll leave you here.

632
00:39:04,280 --> 00:39:05,448
Yes.

633
00:39:06,323 --> 00:39:07,616
Welcome home.

634
00:39:10,995 --> 00:39:12,163
Let's go.

635
00:39:13,789 --> 00:39:16,375
This is amazing, huh?

636
00:39:17,126 --> 00:39:18,711
Nice colors.

637
00:39:18,794 --> 00:39:20,171
‎I picked them.

638
00:39:33,225 --> 00:39:36,896
To be honest, there's something
I haven't told you.

639
00:39:39,482 --> 00:39:43,861
I was offered the chance
to return to headquarters

640
00:39:43,944 --> 00:39:46,238
if I found the spy in Gate 24.

641
00:39:46,906 --> 00:39:51,452
To say I wasn't tempted by the offer
would be a lie.

642
00:39:53,120 --> 00:39:57,750
But I don't think there's
actually a spy in Gate 24.

643
00:39:59,210 --> 00:40:02,505
No. Me neither.

644
00:40:03,130 --> 00:40:07,092
So, I was actually the traitor.

645
00:40:08,052 --> 00:40:09,678
I'm sorry for not telling you.

646
00:40:12,806 --> 00:40:15,351
But you suspecting Seike

647
00:40:15,434 --> 00:40:19,980
led to us solving the case.

648
00:40:21,524 --> 00:40:24,235
So, after accounting for everything,
I say we're even.

649
00:40:26,153 --> 00:40:31,825
You get some extra marks
for being honest with me.

650
00:40:34,870 --> 00:40:37,414
Hey. Where are the passengers
from the Astoria flight?

651
00:40:38,249 --> 00:40:40,167
Ah, they all cleared immigration?

652
00:40:40,251 --> 00:40:41,961
Yes, the last one just left.

653
00:40:42,044 --> 00:40:43,712
We were just a little too late.

654
00:40:43,796 --> 00:40:46,382
What's the matter?
Didn't we solve the case?

655
00:40:46,465 --> 00:40:49,385
No, this is a separate case.

656
00:40:49,468 --> 00:40:53,931
Astoria airport police checked security
footage from the poison incident

657
00:40:54,014 --> 00:40:55,891
and saw a member of Wings of the People
who boarded the flight to Japan.

658
00:40:56,517 --> 00:40:57,810
A member of Wings of the People?

659
00:40:57,893 --> 00:41:00,396
A man suspected of involvement
in the government facility bombing

660
00:41:00,479 --> 00:41:01,897
six months ago

661
00:41:02,481 --> 00:41:04,233
boarded the flight from Astoria.

662
00:41:04,316 --> 00:41:08,279
But was there any Astorian
on the plane who was suspicious?

663
00:41:08,362 --> 00:41:10,322
No, he's not Astorian.

664
00:41:10,406 --> 00:41:11,657
He's Japanese.

665
00:41:12,825 --> 00:41:14,118
This is him.

666
00:41:23,127 --> 00:41:24,461
Hayami…

667
00:41:25,671 --> 00:41:26,672
There's no way…

668
00:41:32,803 --> 00:41:34,096
Shirosaki!

669
00:41:37,433 --> 00:41:41,895
Kazuo Shirosaki, member of Wings
of the People, put the bag on the floor!

670
00:41:56,660 --> 00:41:57,953
Shirosaki.

671
00:42:03,751 --> 00:42:05,628
Shirosaki!

672
00:42:05,711 --> 00:42:09,340
I have nothing to say.

673
00:42:12,593 --> 00:42:13,552
Sorry.

674
00:42:19,058 --> 00:42:21,143
What did you just take?

675
00:42:21,226 --> 00:42:22,102
Hey!

676
00:42:23,687 --> 00:42:25,189
Hurry! Water!

677
00:42:25,272 --> 00:42:26,732
-Call an ambulance!
-Yes!

678
00:42:30,277 --> 00:42:31,362
I saved…

679
00:42:33,405 --> 00:42:34,657
lives all my life.

680
00:42:39,203 --> 00:42:40,663
I'm glad I was…

681
00:42:43,540 --> 00:42:45,209
able to save some more, in the end.

682
00:43:02,017 --> 00:43:03,018
I understand.

683
00:43:08,273 --> 00:43:11,944
Kazuo Shirosaki is unconscious
and in critical condition.

684
00:43:12,820 --> 00:43:15,656
Why would Shirosaki
be in an anti-government group?

685
00:43:17,449 --> 00:43:19,159
He lost his family

686
00:43:19,243 --> 00:43:21,870
in a bombing incident
in Astoria's old town

687
00:43:21,954 --> 00:43:23,539
a year ago.

688
00:43:23,622 --> 00:43:25,249
His family?

689
00:43:26,125 --> 00:43:30,170
Wait. Wasn't the bombing
done by the Wings of the People?

690
00:43:30,254 --> 00:43:32,381
Some reports claim that bombing

691
00:43:33,006 --> 00:43:35,300
was actually carried out
by the government itself.

692
00:43:37,052 --> 00:43:39,263
They say it was a false flag operation

693
00:43:39,847 --> 00:43:41,223
to crack down on dissidents.

694
00:43:41,974 --> 00:43:42,933
That's horrible.

695
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:46,228
-Excuse me.
-Airport Director!

696
00:43:47,020 --> 00:43:48,981
-Come in.
-Arima.

697
00:43:49,606 --> 00:43:51,984
This is Kashiwagi, Cabinet Counselor,

698
00:43:52,067 --> 00:43:53,819
and Arima from the Ministry of Justice.

699
00:43:53,902 --> 00:43:56,405
What's everyone gathering here for?

700
00:43:56,488 --> 00:43:57,573
Actually,

701
00:43:59,241 --> 00:44:01,452
we found this among Kazuo Shirosaki's

702
00:44:03,746 --> 00:44:05,497
personal belongings.

703
00:44:09,334 --> 00:44:11,420
MIDORI SUGIHARA

704
00:44:12,296 --> 00:44:14,923
The Counselor's business card?

705
00:44:16,425 --> 00:44:18,635
We'll need you to hear
what we have to say about this.

706
00:44:19,553 --> 00:44:24,933
You are suspected of having ties
to an anti-government organization.

707
00:44:44,077 --> 00:44:44,995
‎FINAL EPISODE

708
00:44:45,078 --> 00:44:47,456
‎The survival of the gate
‎also hangs on this.

709
00:44:47,539 --> 00:44:48,957
‎You're going to get rid of Gate 24?

710
00:44:49,041 --> 00:44:52,294
‎We received information that there's
‎an assasination plot at the airport.

711
00:44:52,377 --> 00:44:53,754
‎May I see this a second?

712
00:44:53,837 --> 00:44:54,838
‎It's strange.

713
00:44:54,922 --> 00:44:55,797
‎SOS…

714
00:44:55,881 --> 00:44:57,841
‎Is the wife in danger?

715
00:44:57,925 --> 00:44:58,967
‎I need a favor.

716
00:44:59,051 --> 00:45:00,010
‎Subtitle translation by: Maslin Friedman

